{% extends 'guides/_guide_base.html.twig' %}

{% import 'guides/_macros.html.twig' as guide %}

{% block guide_header %}
    <h1 class="guide-hero-title">Average Puzzle Time by Piece Count</h1>

    {% if solo500 and solo1000 %}
        <p class="guide-hero-lead">
            <strong>A 500-piece jigsaw puzzle takes a median {{ guide.duration_words(solo500.medianSeconds) }}, a 1000-piece
            puzzle {{ guide.duration_words(solo1000.medianSeconds) }}</strong> — measured across
            {{ total_solo_solves|number_format }} timed solo solves on MySpeedPuzzling. Here is every standard size from 100
            to 2000 pieces, for one person, a pair and a team.
        </p>
    {% else %}
        <p class="guide-hero-lead">
            Median solve times for every standard size from 100 to 2000 pieces — for one person, a pair and a team —
            measured from real timed solves, not guessed.
        </p>
    {% endif %}
{% endblock %}

{% block guide_content %}
    <p>
        Ask how long a puzzle takes and you usually get a guess. This table is not one: every number is computed from
        solves the speed puzzling community timed and recorded on MySpeedPuzzling, and it refreshes as new solves come
        in. Pick a size to open its own guide with the full breakdown.
    </p>

    {% if rows is not empty %}
        <h2>Puzzle time by piece count</h2>

        <div class="guide-table-card mb-2">
            <div class="table-responsive">
                <table class="table table-striped guide-table mb-0">
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th scope="col">Pieces</th>
                            <th scope="col">Solo median</th>
                            <th scope="col">Solo middle 50%</th>
                            <th scope="col">Pair median</th>
                            <th scope="col">Team median</th>
                            <th scope="col" class="text-end">Solo solves</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        {% for row in rows %}
                            <tr>
                                <th scope="row">
                                    {% if guide_paths[row.pieces] is defined %}
                                        <a href="{{ guide_paths[row.pieces] }}">{{ row.pieces|number_format }} pieces</a>
                                    {% else %}
                                        {{ row.pieces|number_format }} pieces
                                    {% endif %}
                                </th>
                                <td>{{ row.solo.medianSeconds|compactTime }}</td>
                                <td>{{ row.solo.p25Seconds|compactTime }} – {{ row.solo.p75Seconds|compactTime }}</td>
                                <td>{{ row.duo ? row.duo.medianSeconds|compactTime : '–' }}</td>
                                <td>{{ row.team ? row.team.medianSeconds|compactTime : '–' }}</td>
                                <td class="text-end">{{ row.solo.solvesCount|number_format }}</td>
                            </tr>
                        {% endfor %}
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
        </div>
        <p class="text-muted fs-sm">
            Active puzzling time. A pair is two people, a team three or more; "–" means fewer than
            {{ constant('SpeedPuzzling\\Web\\Services\\PuzzleTimeGuides::MINIMUM_GROUP_SOLVES') }} recorded group solves at
            that size, too few for a median. Sizes appear once they have
            {{ constant('SpeedPuzzling\\Web\\Services\\PuzzleTimeGuides::MINIMUM_SOLO_SOLVES') }} recorded solo solves.
        </p>

        {% set smallest = rows|first %}
        {% set largest = rows|last %}

        {% if smallest.pieces != largest.pieces %}
            <h2>Why time grows faster than piece count</h2>

            <p>
                {% set piecesRatio = (largest.pieces / smallest.pieces)|round %}
                {% if largest.solo.medianSecondsPerPiece > smallest.solo.medianSecondsPerPiece %}
                    Going from {{ smallest.pieces }} to {{ largest.pieces|number_format }} pieces multiplies the pieces by
                    {{ piecesRatio }}, but the median time by {{ (largest.solo.medianSeconds / smallest.solo.medianSeconds)|round }}.
                {% endif %}
                At {{ smallest.pieces }} pieces the median solver spends
                {{ smallest.solo.medianSecondsPerPiece|number_format(1) }} seconds per piece; at
                {{ largest.pieces|number_format }} pieces it is {{ largest.solo.medianSecondsPerPiece|number_format(1) }}.
                Every extra piece adds more places to look, sorting takes longer, and on a big puzzle each piece carries a
                smaller part of the picture, so it gives you less to go on.
                {% if solo500 and solo1000 %}
                    The jump from 500 to 1000 pieces shows it best: double the pieces, but
                    {{ (solo1000.medianSeconds / solo500.medianSeconds)|number_format(1) }}&times; the median time.
                {% endif %}
            </p>

            <p>
                Part of the difference is also who solves what. 500 pieces is the size speed puzzlers race, so its median
                reflects a lot of practised solvers; far fewer people time a 1500- or 2000-piece puzzle. Each size's median
                is honest for that size — just keep in mind it describes the puzzlers who solve it.
            </p>
        {% endif %}
    {% endif %}

    <h2>Median, not average — on purpose</h2>

    <p>
        This page answers "what is the average puzzle time", but the numbers are medians: the time half of all solves
        beat. Solve times are lopsided: most solves cluster together, and a long tail of much slower ones — a relaxed
        pace, a hard image — pulls the arithmetic mean upwards. The median ignores how far that tail reaches, so it
        stays a fair "typical" time. The middle 50% column shows how wide the typical range is.
    </p>

    <h2>Solo, in a pair, or as a team</h2>

    <p>
        {% set pairSpeedUps = rows|filter(row => row.duo is not null)|map(row => row.duo.speedUpOver(row.solo)) %}
        {% if pairSpeedUps is not empty %}
            {% if min(pairSpeedUps) > 1 and max(pairSpeedUps) < 2 %}
                A second person makes every size faster, but never twice as fast:
            {% endif %}
            pairs are between {{ min(pairSpeedUps)|number_format(1) }}&times; and
            {{ max(pairSpeedUps)|number_format(1) }}&times; as fast as one person, depending on the size.
        {% endif %}
        How much a partner really saves on 500 and 1000 pieces — and what four people manage — is in
        <a href="{{ path('guide_puzzle_time_pairs_and_teams') }}">how long a 1000-piece puzzle takes with 2 people</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>How we measured this</h2>

    <p>
        We count solves recorded on MySpeedPuzzling as active puzzling time — timed with the built-in
        <a href="{{ path('stopwatch') }}">stopwatch</a>, which pauses during breaks, or entered by hand. We leave out solves
        flagged as implausible, times faster than 0.6 seconds per piece, and solves timed from the unopened box, and we
        compute every size separately. A pair or team time is recorded once per group. The
        <a href="{{ path('methodology') }}">methodology page</a> explains how MySpeedPuzzling computes its community
        statistics.
    </p>

    <h2>Find out where you fit</h2>

    <p>
        Time your next puzzle with the free <a href="{{ path('stopwatch') }}">MySpeedPuzzling stopwatch</a>, then look it
        up in the <a href="{{ path('puzzles') }}">puzzle database</a> to compare your time with everyone who solved the
        same puzzle. The classic 1000-piece puzzle has its own in-depth guide:
        <a href="{{ path('guide_puzzle_time_by_pieces') }}">how long does a 1000-piece puzzle take?</a>
    </p>
{% endblock %}
